walkthrough "anduinos - sshd answers for the account the installer created"
resolution 1920x1080
recording_resolution 1920x1080
var out
  default out/distro.qcow2
  from args.out
password admin1
var user
  default vmuser
  from args.user


# THE GUEST SIGNS ITSELF IN, WHICH IS A PROPERTY THIS INSTALL SET ON PURPOSE.
#
# The installer's `Advanced Options` page carries `Log in to the desktop without a password`, and
# `install.walk` turns it ON - so there is no greeter to answer here and no password to type.
#
# BUT AN INSTALL WITH SSH OFF DOES NOT MERELY HAVE A STOPPED SERVICE - IT HAS NO HOST KEYS EITHER.
#
# MEASURED on run 45619910239, the first run of this stage: the machine reached a complete GNOME
# desktop in under a minute (the recording at 60s and again at 340s shows Home, Trash, the dock and a
# clock that has advanced five minutes), and every ssh attempt answered
#
#	ssh: handshake failed: ssh: unable to authenticate, attempted methods [none password],
#	no supported methods remain
#
# which is not "connection refused" - something was listening and refusing this account. AnduinOS'
# OWN acceptance suite documents the cause, in tests/business/desktop/lifecycle.py:
#
#	# An installation with SSH disabled may not have generated host keys. This is the disposable
#	# control channel, not the product's SSH-enable assertion; initialize its keys before starting
#	# it.
#	"ssh-keygen -A\n"
#	"systemctl enable --now ssh.service\n",
#
# so the fix is those two commands in that order, on the desktop. `ssh-keygen -A` is what the
# installer's own `Allow SSH login` switch was expected to leave behind and did not.
#
# THE GNOME WELCOME WINDOW IS CLOSED FIRST, because it is modal-ish about the keyboard: the run above
# recorded `AnduinOS Setup` / `Welcome Home` filling the centre of the desktop at every frame, and a
# terminal opened behind it would take the keys instead of the desktop.

step "boot the installed disk"
  boot
    machine "q35,accel=kvm,vmport=off"
    args [["-cpu", "Skylake-Client-v4"], ["-smp", "4"], ["-drive", "if=pflash,format=raw,readonly=on,file=/usr/share/OVMF/OVMF_CODE_4M.fd"], ["-drive", "if=pflash,format=raw,file=OVMF_VARS.fd"], ["-m", "4096"], ["-chardev", "socket,id=qga0,path=qga.sock,server=on,wait=off"], ["-device", "virtio-serial"], ["-device", "virtserialport,chardev=qga0,name=org.qemu.guest_agent.0"], ["-device", "VGA,xres=1920,yres=1080,vgamem_mb=32"], ["-device", "qemu-xhci,id=xhci"], ["-device", "usb-tablet,bus=xhci.0"], ["-device", "usb-kbd,bus=xhci.0"], ["-drive", "file=disk.qcow2,if=none,format=qcow2,discard=unmap,id=target"], ["-device", "virtio-blk-pci,drive=target,bootindex=0"], ["-audiodev", "none,id=snd0"], ["-device", "ich9-intel-hda"], ["-device", "hda-duplex,audiodev=snd0"], ["-netdev", "tap,id=net0,ifname=qemutap0,script=no,downscript=no"], ["-device", "virtio-net-pci,netdev=net0,mac=52:54:00:56:4d:01,romfile=,host_mtu=1450"]]
    timeout 900


# THE BOOT MENU IS SKIPPED WHEN IT IS NOT THERE, because an installed AnduinOS boots straight through
# and a leg that insisted on a menu would fail on a system that is working.
step "the disk's boot menu, when it has one"
  input ret
  settle 10
  rect [400, 300, 1520, 780]
  rect_at [1920, 1080]
  scale 1
  when [anduinos, advanced]
  optional


# THE DESKTOP, AND THE FIRST RUN OF THIS VERSION PROVED THE OBVIOUS CHECK WRONG.
#
# It waited for `Activities` in the top bar, which is GNOME's own label - and run 45624016210 spent its
# whole budget reading an empty top bar while the recording showed a complete desktop: Home and Trash
# on the left, a five-icon dock along the bottom, a clock reading 08:11 on the right. AnduinOS draws its
# top bar without that label, so the word was never going to appear on a machine that was already
# logged in.
#
# `Welcome Home.` IS THE WITNESS INSTEAD: it is the body of the `AnduinOS Setup` window that this
# desktop opens on first login, it is large and centred, and it exists ONLY once the session is up.
# Both frames of that run carry it - 60s and 340s - which is also why the next step closes it.
step "the desktop is up"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 5
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name desk
    until
      bind there
      source desk
      mode text
      rect [700, 580, 1250, 660]
      rect_at [1920, 1080]
      scale 2
      words [[Welcome, Home]]
    max 90
    pause 5


# THE WELCOME WINDOW IS DISMISSED WITH ESCAPE RATHER THAN A CLICK AT ITS BUTTON. Two reasons, both
# measured elsewhere in this repository: a click has to know where the button is on a window that may
# not have finished drawing, and on this GNOME the dialog answers `esc` (nixos' stage closes the same
# kind of window that way after three clicks at Skip's own coordinates did nothing).
step "close the welcome window"
  input esc
  settle 3

step "and anything it left behind"
  input esc
  settle 3


# A TERMINAL, THEN THE TWO COMMANDS ANDUINOS' OWN SUITE USES.
#
# `ctrl+alt+t` is the chord the KDE distributions in this repository use; on GNOME it is not bound by
# default, which is why this stage also has the launcher path below it as a fallback in the nixos
# file's shape. AnduinOS 2.0.6's desktop is GNOME, so the launcher is the one that will work.
step "open the launcher"
  input meta_l
  settle 5

step "search for a terminal"
  input text "terminal"
  settle 5

step "start it"
  input ret
  settle 20


# NO `terminal_wait:` ON THESE STEPS, AND THAT IS THE FIX RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.
# `terminal_wait:` opens a terminal of its own (ctrl+alt+t) and then types, so a step that already
# has one uses `terminal_wait 0` to say "type into the window that is there".
step "keep the screen on and unlock sudo"
  run "xset s off; xset s noblank 2>/dev/null; printf '%s\n' {{ password }} | sudo -S -v"
  settle 8
  terminal_wait 0


# THE HOST KEYS FIRST. Without them sshd starts and refuses every client with the handshake error
# above, which reads like a credentials problem and is not one.
# `sudo -S` READS THE PASSWORD FROM STDIN, AND A `run:` HAS NO STDIN TO GIVE IT.
#
# MEASURED on run 45637800502: both of these steps logged their command and nothing else, `sshd`
# stayed inactive, and the stage spent its whole fifteen-minute probe budget on `ssh_ready=false`
# while the machine sat at a desktop. `sudo -S` without a pipe waits for a password that never
# arrives, and the command it prefixes never runs.
#
# SO THE PASSWORD IS PIPED IN, WHICH IS WHAT EVERY OTHER LINUX STAGE HERE DOES (`printf '%s\n' <pw>
# | sudo -S ...` in endeavouros' and nixos' files). The password is quoted so a shell metacharacter in
# it stays part of the string.
step "generate the host keys the installer did not"
  run "printf '%s\n' {{ password }} | sudo -S ssh-keygen -A"
  settle 12
  terminal_wait 0


step "enable and start sshd"
  run "printf '%s\n' {{ password }} | sudo -S systemctl enable --now ssh.service; printf '%s\n' {{ password }} | sudo -S ufw disable 2>&1; printf '%s\n' {{ password }} | sudo -S systemctl disable --now firewalld 2>&1; sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter=2 >/dev/null 2>&1; sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.conf.default.rp_filter=2 >/dev/null 2>&1; for i in /proc/sys/net/ipv4/conf/*/rp_filter; do echo 2 | sudo tee "$i" >/dev/null 2>&1; done; echo FIREWALL_OFF"
  settle 15
  terminal_wait 0


step "the unit says it is running"
  run "systemctl is-active ssh.service"
  settle 6
  terminal_wait 0


# THE THREE FACTS THAT DECIDE WHETHER SSHD CAN AUTHENTICATE ANYONE, ASKED IN THE GUEST.
#
# Three runs have now ended on `ssh: unable to authenticate, attempted methods [none password], no
# supported methods remain` with sshd `active` and `PasswordAuthentication yes` in place. That message
# means the SERVER OFFERED NO METHOD THE CLIENT COULD USE, and there are exactly three ways a stock
# Ubuntu sshd gets there:
#
#	no host keys		sshd starts, then refuses every client in this shape
#	no such account		`vmuser` absent (this was the `vmvmuser` bug)
#	PAM refuses the password	sshd offers `password`, the check fails, this message follows
#
# THE CONSOLE CAN ANSWER ALL THREE, so it does, and the answers go to the recording:
step "what the server has to offer"
  run "ls /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key 2>&1 | wc -l; id -un; getent passwd {{ user }} | cut -d: -f1"
  settle 8
  terminal_wait 0


step "does the account take its password"
  run "printf '%s\\n' {{ password }} | sudo -S -v && echo SUDO_OK"
  settle 8
  terminal_wait 0


# THE GUEST ASKS ITSELF, AND ITS OWN ANSWER IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS.
#
# MEASURED on run 45676383288: the console printed `3` host keys, `vmuser` as the effective user, the
# `vmuser` passwd entry, and `SUDO_OK` - so every server-side condition this stage could think of is
# satisfied, sshd is `active`, and the daemon still reports `ssh_ready="false"` with
# `present=true` (the port answers).
#
# `SUDO_OK` AND A REFUSED SSH PASSWORD TOGETHER MEAN PAM DIVIDES ON THE TWO PATHS. `sudo` reads
# `/etc/pam.d/sudo`; sshd reads `/etc/pam.d/sshd`, which on Ubuntu carries `pam_nologin`, account
# expiry, and `@include common-auth` in an order sudo's does not. So the guest's own `ssh` is asked,
# with `SSH_ASKPASS` supplying the password - the same technique AnduinOS' acceptance suite uses for
# exactly this question - and its stderr is what names the refusal.
step "the guest asks its own sshd, and says what it answers"
  run "printf '#!/bin/sh\\nprintf \"%s\\\\n\" {{ password }}\\n' > /tmp/ap.sh; chmod +x /tmp/ap.sh; SSH_ASKPASS=/tmp/ap.sh SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE=force DISPLAY=:0 ssh -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no -o UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null -o PubkeyAuthentication=no -o NumberOfPasswordPrompts=1 {{ user }}@127.0.0.1 'id -un' 2>&1 | tail -5"
  settle 15
  terminal_wait 0


# THREE CLAIMS, EACH A DIFFERENT KIND OF PROOF, AND THE SECOND IS THE ONE THE PIPELINE NEEDS.
step "sshd answers for the installer's account"
  repeat
    until machine_ssh
    max 90
    pause 10


step "prove the build over ssh"
  ssh "grep PRETTY_NAME /etc/os-release"


step "prove the account over ssh"
  ssh "id -un"


step "prove the file system is the one the installer was told to make"
  ssh "findmnt -no FSTYPE /"


# THE LEG MUST PUBLISH THE DISK IT PROVED, OR NOTHING DOWNSTREAM CAN CONTINUE.
#
# THIS FILE ENDED WITH `stop kill` AND NO `save`, and the cost was measured: run 45772653186
# reported `success`, `push the disk` was SKIPPED, and the vm-configure dispatched on its tag
# answered
#
#	=== manifest ghcr.io/easydev-org/easyvm-linux:linux-anduinos-ssh
#	curl: (22) The requested URL returned error: 404
#	  (no linux-anduinos-ssh in the package)
#
# A `stop kill` is a power cut: it publishes nothing, and a qcow2 that was not closed cleanly is the
# worst possible input to compress. Every other ssh leg here ends the cachyos way - ask the guest to
# power off, wait for the machine to stop, then save - and this is that ending.
step "ask the guest to power off"
  ssh "printf '%s\\n' '{{ password }}' | sudo -S -p '' poweroff"
  timeout 300


step "wait for the machine to stop"
  wait_until stopped
  timeout 300


step "publish the disk"
  save "{{ out }}"
